<article class="env-lemma" id="04SK">
       
      
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04SK">Lemma <span data-tag="04SK">80.11.1</span>.</a> Let $S$ be a scheme. Let $F : (\mathit{Sch}/S)_{fppf}^{opp} \to \textit{Sets}$ be a functor. Let $\{ S_ i \to S\} _{i \in I}$ be a covering of $(\mathit{Sch}/S)_{fppf}$. Assume that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$F$ is a sheaf, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
each $F_ i = h_{S_ i} \times F$ is an algebraic space, and 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\coprod _{i \in I} F_ i$ is an algebraic space (see Spaces, Lemma <a href="/tag/02WQ" data-tag="02WQ">65.8.4</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Then $F$ is an algebraic space. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      Consider the morphism $\coprod F_ i \to F$. This is the base change of $\coprod S_ i \to S$ via $F \to S$. Hence it is representable, locally of finite presentation, flat and surjective by our definition of an fppf covering and Lemma <a href="/tag/046F" data-tag="046F">80.4.2</a>. Thus Theorem <a href="/tag/04S6" data-tag="04S6">80.10.1</a> applies to show that $F$ is an algebraic space. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>